{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017PVTM4K3tPH7axV8uYm9zB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd039-env\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/env: Run a Command in a Modified Environment\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Shell scripts and build systems frequently need to run commands with a controlled set of\n6\t  environment variables, adding, removing, or replacing entries before exec. The macOS BSD\n7\t  env lacks GNU extensions such as -C (chdir) and -S (split string), but the core flags\n8\t  (-i, -u, NAME=VALUE) are shared. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n9\t  reference binary (genv, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all supported flag\n10\t  combinations and edge cases, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n11\t\n12\tgoals:\n13\t  - G1: Print the current environment when invoked with no arguments and no COMMAND.\n14\t  - G2: Run COMMAND in a modified environment, inheriting, unsetting, or overriding variables as directed by flags and NAME=VALUE pairs.\n15\t  - G3: Support starting from an empty environment (-i) to give full control over what COMMAND inherits.\n16\t  - G4: Support NUL-delimited output (-0) for safe parsing of values that contain newlines.\n17\t\n18\trequirements:\n19\t  R1:\n20\t    title: Default behavior\n21\t    items:\n22\t      - R1.1:\n23\t          text: When invoked with no arguments, must print every environment variable on its own line in NAME=VALUE format and exit 0.\n24\t      - R1.2:\n25\t          text: When invoked with COMMAND [ARG ...], must execute COMMAND with the resulting environment after all modifications have been applied.\n26\t      - R1.3:\n27\t          text: If COMMAND is not found, must exit 127. If COMMAND is found but cannot be executed, must exit 126.\n28\t\n29\t  R2:\n30\t    title: Environment modification\n31\t    items:\n32\t      - R2.1:\n33\t          text: -i or --ignore-environment must start COMMAND with an empty environment. Only explicitly supplied NAME=VALUE pairs appear in the child process environment.\n34\t      - R2.2:\n35\t          text: -u NAME or --unset=NAME must remove the variable NAME from the environment before COMMAND runs. Multiple -u flags must be supported.\n36\t      - R2.3:\n37\t          text: Each NAME=VALUE argument (positional, before COMMAND) must set or override the variable NAME in the environment. The first argument that does not contain '=' and is not a flag is the start of COMMAND.\n38\t\n39\t  R3:\n40\t    title: Output formatting and exit codes\n41\t    items:\n42\t      - R3.1:\n43\t          text: -0 or --null must terminate each output line with a NUL byte (0x00) instead of a newline when printing the environment (no COMMAND case).\n44\t      - R3.2:\n45\t          text: When COMMAND is executed, env must exit with the same exit code as COMMAND.\n46\t      - R3.3:\n47\t          text: Must print an error message to stderr when an invalid option is given and exit 125.\n48\t\n49\t  R4:\n50\t    title: Differential testing\n51\t    items:\n52\t      - R4.1:\n53\t          text: Differential tests must compare stdout output and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary (genv) via pkg/testutils.\n54\t      - R4.2:\n55\t          text: \"Tests must cover: no-argument environment dump, NAME=VALUE setting, -u unsetting, -i empty environment, -0 NUL-delimited output, COMMAND execution with modified env, and exit code passthrough.\"\n56\t      - R4.3:\n57\t          text: Tests must verify error output and exit code for invalid options and missing commands.\n58\t\n59\tnon_goals:\n60\t  - cmd/env does not implement -C (change directory) or -S (split string) GNU extensions.\n61\t  - cmd/env does not read variable assignments from stdin; all input comes from command-line arguments.\n62\t  - cmd/env does not perform shell expansion on COMMAND or its arguments; exec is direct.\n63\t\n64\tacceptance_criteria:\n65\t  - id: AC1\n66\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/env with no arguments produces the same output as genv with no arguments.\"\n67\t    traces:\n68\t      - R1.1\n69\t      - R1.2\n70\t      - R1.3\n71\t  - id: AC2\n72\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/env FOO=bar env prints an environment that includes FOO=bar, matching genv.\"\n73\t    traces:\n74\t      - R1.1\n75\t      - R1.2\n76\t      - R1.3\n77\t  - id: AC3\n78\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/env -i FOO=bar env produces only FOO=bar (plus any exec-injected variables), matching genv -i.\"\n79\t    traces:\n80\t      - R2.1\n81\t      - R4.2\n82\t  - id: AC4\n83\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/env -u HOME env omits HOME from the output, matching genv -u HOME.\"\n84\t    traces:\n85\t      - R2.2\n86\t      - R4.2\n87\t  - id: AC5\n88\t    criterion: \"Differential test against genv passes for environment dump, -i, -u, NAME=VALUE, -0, COMMAND execution, and exit code passthrough.\"\n89\t    traces:\n90\t      - R1.1\n91\t      - R1.3\n92\t      - R2.1\n93\t      - R2.2\n94\t      - R3.2\n95\t      - R3.3\n96\t      - R4.1\n97\t      - R4.2\n98\t      - R4.3\n99\t  - id: AC6\n100\t    criterion: \"cmd/env compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n101\t    traces:\n102\t      - R1.1\n103\t      - R1.2\n104\t      - R1.3\n105\t      - R2.1\n106\t      - R2.2\n107\t      - R2.3\n108\t      - R3.1\n109\t      - R3.2\n110\t      - R3.3\n111\t      - R4.1\n112\t      - R4.2\n113\t      - R4.3\n114\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01R8Sqior9J3KXE68XdFuju6","session_id":"d461bfc8-ec86-4b76-a705-0ddc369f10bf","uuid":"2440bdb2-6695-4c88-8f1d-807ce9c97729","timestamp":"2026-05-20T13:55:40.323Z"}
